 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "sotah.40b.8",
   "daf": "40b",
   "sentenceId": 7,
   "he": "וְכֹהֵן גָּדוֹל עוֹמֵד וּמְקַבֵּל וְקוֹרֵא וְכוּ׳. ״עוֹמֵד״ — מִכְּלָל דְּיוֹשֵׁב הוּא? וְהָאָמַר מָר: אֵין יְשִׁיבָה בַּעֲזָרָה אֶלָּא לְמַלְכֵי בֵּית דָּוִד בִּלְבַד, שֶׁנֶּאֱמַר: ״וַיָּבֹא הַמֶּלֶךְ דָּוִד וַיֵּשֶׁב לִפְנֵי ה׳ וַיֹּאמֶר מִי אָנֹכִי וְגוֹ׳״!",
   "en": "It is stated in the mishna: «And the High Priest stands, and receives» the Torah scroll, «and reads» from it. The Gemara asks: From the fact that he stands, it can be understood «by inference» that until that point «he» had been «sitting. But didn’t the Master say» (Tosefta, Sanhedrin 4:4) that «sitting in» the Temple «courtyard» is permitted «only for kings from the house of David, as it is stated: “Then King David went in, and sat before the Lord; and he said: Who am I,» O Lord God, and what is my house, that You have brought me thus far?” (II Samuel 7:18)."
  }
 ],
